WEBVTT

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:48.360
Hertfordshire - the treasure of the Home Counties boasts some of the finest farmland in the country.

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:57.720
In the summer, these fields light up as the oilseed rape crop flowers.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:06.080
I'll be meeting the farming family who are pressing their own gold from this crop and the chef who's embracing rapeseed oil's healthy properties.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:12.080
This will only have a quarter of the saturated fat of any olive oil on the planet.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.800
And, here in Hertfordshire, the woodland runs thick and lush through the landscape.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:19.880
This is Heartwood Forest.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:31.920
It's a mere youngster compared to the ancient woodlands in the county, but, like its older and more established cousins, it's already thriving and bursting with life.

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:36.120
Tom's waiting for a bus that may never come.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:47.080
I'll be finding out about cuts to rural transport and asking if we can continue to support bus services in some of the remotest parts of our countryside.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:52.960
And Adam's catching up with one of the most inspirational farmers he's ever met.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:02:09.199
At 83, she's still running the family farm, coming to markets and going strong and, last year, her lifetime achievements were recognised when she won the Countryfile Farming Hero award.

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:12.120
And later in the programme, I'll be asking for your nominations for this year's awards.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:32.640
Hertfordshire - graceful open countryside, ancient woodlands and sweeping skies.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:40.520
Less than an hour from central London lies this rural county, rich in prime agricultural land.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:44.920
I'm visiting a farm in the village of Wilstone, just north of Tring on the western edge of the Chilterns.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:59.320
Today, I'm going in search of a crop that sets our landscape ablaze with colour every summertime.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:07.680
Rapeseed - in recent decades, fields of gold have become a familiar sight in our countryside.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:15.520
Simon Mead's family have been growing rapeseed since the 1980s.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:22.240
Eight years ago, they began transforming its tiny black grains into the liquid gold of rapeseed oil.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:30.880
Now, obviously, Simon, when you're growing this yourself and you're trying to produce the finest oil you can, protection is the key and that's why we're carrying this stuff here.

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:35.520
Yes, Matt, the pigeons are starting to become a bit of a nuisance.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:40.760
They've eaten all the beech moss up in the Chiltern Hills and this is the next crop on the menu.

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:47.720
In a hard winter, when there's not much else for the pigeons to eat, they can reduce even a crop like this down to ground level.

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:51.160
So we're going to set some up and see if we can scare them off a bit.

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:51.640
So, these are bird scarers.

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:56.680
Basically, you just light the end of the rope and, as time progresses, the bangers go off.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:02.360
Yeah, every half an hour they go off and it scares them off.

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.000
Establishing this crop initially is quite a tricky process, Simon.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:14.960
Yes, it gets planted in August and it's in the ground all the way through to the following July, so it's in the ground for 11 months.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:23.720
There's lots of opportunity for things to go wrong, but once it's up and away like this crop here, we don't seem to have many problems.

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:28.960
In the spring, rapeseed produces yellow flowers that turn into seed pods.

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:33.360
By midsummer, the pods have dried out and the seeds turned black.

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:35.480
It's these seeds which are pressed to create the oil.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:44.080
We get about three and a half to four and a half tonnes a hectare, which is about the size of a football pitch.

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:47.120
That should produce about 2,000-2,500 bottles off a hectare.

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:50.000
So, off this field, we're getting about 32,000 bottles.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:59.160
OK, right, let's get this sorted out so you can get rid of these pigeons.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:01.920
That's it so we'd better get out the way.

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:04.560
Yes.

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:05.960
There must be 300 or so up there.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:06.920
I saw a flock as I came in.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:28.200
Once harvested, the rapeseed grains are dried and stored, then cold-pressed and bottled on the farm.

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:31.320
Right, so it's from your fields, through the press and into here.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:32.240
It's like a science lab.

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:35.280
Yeah, it's all been filtered before it gets here, Matt.

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:37.600
And this is Alex, your son.

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:40.120
That looks like quite a tricky job, you've got to be quick, eh?

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:50.560
'As well as making pure rapeseed oil, 'Simon has recently started using the oil to create a new product.'

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:57.400
Right, so the seasoning and the sugar has already gone in.

00:05:56.440 --> 00:06:01.000
This is a bowlful of mustard and we're in the process of making some mayonnaise, then, Simon.

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:04.880
Yes, it's a natural progression to go down, Matt.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:05.160
There's lots of oil in it.

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:09.280
70% of a good mayonnaise is oil.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:13.360
Has it been quite a steep learning curve?

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:19.400
We split a few batches before we got it right.

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:19.960
Next is egg yolk.

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:21.640
Yes, a free-range egg yolk.

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:24.120
Right, so that's all the ingredients in there?

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:24.360
Yes, all the main ingredients.

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:25.560
All we've got to do now is introduce the oil slowly.

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:39.960
Ow!

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:41.480
That's, erm...

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:41.720
That's mustardy at the moment!

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:46.200
Let's get it all mixed up nicely before we start introducing the oil.

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:54.160
Oh, yeah, look at the difference.

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:54.800
You can stir the outside into the middle, Matt.

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:55.800
Yeah.

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:56.640
You've made a nice mayonnaise there, Matt, well done.

00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:57.040
Happy, yeah?

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:09.440
And there you have it - my very first jar of mayonnaise.

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:20.320
'Even the leftover seed husks 'are a useful by-product from the oil making.'

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:30.080
Once the rapeseed has been through the pressing process, nothing goes to waste, as these cows and Simon's cousin Chris will now demonstrate.

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:31.840
There you go, Chris.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:32.200
Thank you very much.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:33.800
So there's still a lot of goodness left in this waste from the oil process.

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:36.840
Yes, there is.

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:40.120
It's very high in protein.

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:53.640
The protein builds muscle and the oiliness of the feed creates very little dust so it's less irritating to the cow's nose and throat.

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:57.800
There you go, Matt, you have a go.

00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:07.320
'These cows certainly seem to enjoy it, and later, I'll be finding out 'if the oil goes down as well with the customers of Simon's farm shop.'

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:23.680
Now, rural buses are a lifeline for many people, but, as Tom's been finding out, it's claimed that more and more villages are being cut off by the loss of local services.

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:37.760
Deep in the heart of the countryside, you can wait a long time for a bus.

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:39.440
A very, VERY long time.

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:50.160
And, with councils all over the UK cutting millions from their transport budgets, the wait could get even longer.

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:53.920
And for some rural communities, the bus might not come at all.

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:59.960
Indeed, the one I'm waiting for, after March, is going to be scrapped.

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:19.360
'One of the areas hardest hit is here in Yorkshire, 'where 146 bus services have been cut, 'altered or withdrawn in the past two years.

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:22.880
'It's a similar picture across the country.'

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:31.640
In total, over 1,000 supported bus services in England and Wales have been affected since 2014.

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:38.720
And, according to the Campaign For Better Transport, it's hitting rural areas the hardest.

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:45.840
In the past, it's had a devastating effect on the elderly, but now, increasingly, it's affecting the young.

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:50.440
Robyn Conmee is an apprentice wedding planner.

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:54.760
With no bus services connecting her rural Lancashire village, her journey to work is harder than most.

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:57.680
Tell me about your struggle to actually get in to work.

00:09:57.560 --> 00:10:00.800
Well, it's quite hard with the timings for work.

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:06.200
Because I live quite rurally, now and again, I have been late for work, which has got me in a bit of trouble before now, yeah.

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:06.840
It's quite a journey, especially if I get the bus.

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:11.400
It's about a 30-minute walk and then a six-minute bus journey, so, yeah, it takes a lot of time.

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:16.520
It's just a shame that we haven't got a bus service that runs from where I live.

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:17.360
It's quite frustrating.

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:19.440
Well, life isn't just about work.

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:20.400
What about your social life?

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:23.040
How does transport affect that?

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:23.560
It really affects it.

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:25.560
My friends live further out than I do.

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:29.000
It's a case of getting there, it's just easier to say, "Oh, I'll give this one a miss."

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:34.960
Robyn is not alone in being at the mercy of public transport.

00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:44.120
That's because nearly two thirds of job-seekers either have no access to a vehicle or cannot drive.

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:58.000
With more and more bus services being cut, hotel boss Carol Sleet is finding many young apprentices are being put off from working by the cost of finding other transport.

00:11:06.440 --> 00:11:07.760
There we go.

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:08.400
Wow, looks fantastic!

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:10.160
I get served by the boss, too.

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:11.400
It's not going to happen often.

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:15.160
So, tell me, how tricky is it for your business getting young staff?

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:15.920
It's really tricky.

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:18.760
Young people just can't afford it.

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:19.320
It's so expensive.

00:11:18.520 --> 00:11:22.160
The buses are really expensive, taxis are even more expensive.

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:31.520
The apprenticeship wage is, I think, £132 a week, and, when you're asking people to spend, it can be nearly £100 a week in transport.

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:34.840
They think to themselves, "What's the point of going in to work?"

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:36.880
And you're actually finding that, are you?

00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:41.400
Do you have examples of places where you've got the spaces to work if you've got the people willing, but joining the two up...

00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:50.800
I have an apprenticeship place in every single one of our venues that I have to practically beg to find somebody to come and work in them.

00:11:49.960 --> 00:12:05.640
For young people living in rural areas, buses can provide both independence and the ability to work, and for the elderly, they're a lifeline from isolation and loneliness.

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:20.120
But, across the country, buses have been hit by a vicious cycle of falling passenger numbers, rising fares and cuts to services - a process exacerbated by severe reductions in local authority budgets.

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:31.400
In recent years, getting on for £50 million has been cut from supported bus funding across the UK, although the figures from Northern Ireland do include trains.

00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:37.480
And, just in England, 11 local authorities aren't spending anything at all.

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:39.280
So, what is the impact on rural communities?

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:43.600
Good morning, Martin.

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:43.800
How are you doing?

00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:45.480
Taking advantage of the local produce while you're here?

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:46.400
Absolutely.

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:47.920
What have you got there?

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:50.080
Some Wensleydale.

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:51.600
This is Kit Calvert, this is made just up the road.

00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:52.040
That's the top of the range.

00:12:51.240 --> 00:12:52.480
That's good.

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:54.680
Thanks very much.

00:12:53.920 --> 00:12:55.160
Thanks, Steve.

00:12:54.320 --> 00:12:56.600
Bye-bye.

00:12:57.360 --> 00:13:03.120
'Martin Abrams is from the Campaign For Better Transport.

00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:12.280
'He believes that, 'despite commercial operators stepping in 'to run some profitable bus services, 'important isolated routes have disappeared completely.'

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:19.800
You say local authorities have cut their budgets, but haven't those just been replaced by commercial operators and maybe there's been no loss?

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:21.600
Well, no, there hasn't.

00:13:20.840 --> 00:13:28.920
Over the past decade, supported bus services, those funded by local authorities, have lost about 55 million miles.

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:36.920
In the same timeframe, commercial operators have only increased their operations by about 13 million miles so they haven't picked up the overall shortfall in the loss of supported services.

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:40.640
So you think money spent in public transport is a good investment?

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:42.160
Absolutely.

00:13:41.480 --> 00:13:43.280
It's a necessary investment.

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:50.360
For every £1 that's invested in buses, they generate up to £5 in wider economic benefits.

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:59.840
We're here in Leyburn on a busy, bustling market day and these towns really rely on bus services to get people to the market, to get people to spend their money in their local economy.

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:02.000
If you take services away, then it's places like this that are going to be hit really hard.

00:14:03.040 --> 00:14:11.200
So, along with the obvious environmental benefits, the economic benefits of bus services appear to stack up too.

00:14:12.280 --> 00:14:22.320
As rural buses are cut back, some people risk being cut off from jobs, services and shops.

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:24.880
But it's not bad news everywhere.

00:14:24.040 --> 00:14:29.600
As I'll be finding out later, some rural communities are joining together to run the service for themselves.

00:14:33.560 --> 00:14:44.640
Mighty and magnificent, these natural skyscrapers - our trees - make us feel very small.

00:14:47.400 --> 00:14:56.040
We're so used to looking up at the canopy that it's easy to think they've always been here, towering over us.

00:14:58.360 --> 00:15:07.440
But they were young once - small and fragile and barely recognisable as trees.

00:15:07.480 --> 00:15:14.720
This is just a baby forest, but it's destined for great things.

00:15:14.040 --> 00:15:23.680
Each of these spindly saplings is a native British species and there's everything here from oak to hornbeam, birch and willow.

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:27.960
It's the largest new native forest in Britain and it's called Heartwood.

00:15:29.680 --> 00:15:39.560
By the time it's finished, there will be more than half a million trees covering 850 acres of former arable land.

00:15:38.600 --> 00:15:41.760
I'm particularly excited to be here because it's not my first visit to Heartwood Forest.

00:15:40.840 --> 00:15:46.600
'Back in 2011, I helped plant some of these trees.'

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:52.000
Here we've got an oak for you.

00:15:51.160 --> 00:15:53.560
These are supposed to be the hardest ones, aren't they?

00:15:52.600 --> 00:15:53.960
They can be.

00:15:53.240 --> 00:15:54.640
Some of the roots are quite big on them.

00:15:57.880 --> 00:16:05.480
'Louise Neicho from the Woodland Trust has been in charge 'from the moment the first tree was planted.'

00:16:05.480 --> 00:16:07.920
Hi, Louise, it's good to see you again.

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:08.680
Hi, welcome back to Heartwood.

00:16:08.120 --> 00:16:09.920
Thank you very much.

00:16:09.080 --> 00:16:10.320
It's been a while!

00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:13.600
In fact, I thought I might recognise this, but it's changed so much.

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:15.280
It has changed.

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:18.280
We've actually planted half a million trees since the last time you were here.

00:16:17.800 --> 00:16:19.160
That's amazing.

00:16:18.200 --> 00:16:21.760
Your aim was 600,000.

00:16:21.400 --> 00:16:25.000
Yeah, so obviously 100,000 still to go, still a lot of work to do.

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:26.800
That's remarkable.

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:29.600
'I want to see how the trees I planted are coming along.'

00:16:31.880 --> 00:16:36.080
This was the area we planted.

00:16:35.280 --> 00:16:36.600
Could it be this one?

00:16:35.680 --> 00:16:37.120
It could be!

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:37.960
Oh, look at that.

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:40.320
It's still growing.

00:16:39.520 --> 00:16:41.520
Tell Ellie the truth.

00:16:41.200 --> 00:16:42.520
It's not mine!

00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:44.080
Mine died!

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:46.520
I knew it!

00:16:45.560 --> 00:16:48.360
I didn't know what to say!

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:50.120
Which one is mine?

00:16:49.320 --> 00:16:50.560
Is it that gap?

00:16:49.600 --> 00:16:50.880
It's the gap!

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:51.200
Oh, no!

00:16:50.240 --> 00:16:51.440
I can't bear it.

00:16:50.480 --> 00:16:54.920
There's a lovely big gap in my memory, that's nice!

00:16:54.000 --> 00:17:03.440
But when you're planting a forest on this scale, 350 hectares, when you've got little gaps like this, that is absolutely fine.

00:17:03.200 --> 00:17:10.480
'I do have a chance to redeem myself, though.

00:17:09.680 --> 00:17:13.400
'Every single tree in Heartwood Forest is planted by a volunteer.

00:17:12.680 --> 00:17:19.839
'34,000 people have been involved since the project began in 2009 'and a hardcore bunch are out today in force.

00:17:21.599 --> 00:17:25.640
'They're creating an arboretum.

00:17:24.839 --> 00:17:32.960
'It will showcase 10,000 native British trees, 'all of which are being planted this winter.'

00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:37.240
I'm just popping a nice oak tree in here.

00:17:36.600 --> 00:17:38.000
Oh, lovely!

00:17:37.240 --> 00:17:39.720
What are you going to do?

00:17:38.760 --> 00:17:40.720
I'm going to go right next to you here.

00:17:39.880 --> 00:17:41.440
Good.

00:17:40.760 --> 00:17:45.240
And what about doing all this outdoor work in winter?

00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:45.960
Do you not mind it?

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:49.600
Oh, no, no, no, so long as it's not TOO awful.

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:57.760
No, we've got waterproof skins and we're built for outdoors if you wrap up warm and it's lovely to do something that's useful for the world.

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:04.320
'Only six years into its life and people are already getting 'so much out of Heartwood Forest.'

00:18:07.920 --> 00:18:10.400
And wildlife is too.

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:19.760
Forests, even young ones, are incredible habitats and, if you're up early enough, there's a good chance you'll see some of the animals that have made Heartwood their home.

00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:23.040
'It's early.

00:18:22.520 --> 00:18:28.360
The sun will soon be rising, 'but I'm going to see what creatures the night has left behind.'

00:18:31.560 --> 00:18:46.000
As the land turns from arable fields to forest, it's really important to understand how the environment is changing and the small animals in that ecosystem can tell us an awful lot.

00:18:47.480 --> 00:18:48.800
The trap has dropped, has it?

00:18:47.960 --> 00:18:57.920
'Ian Flack and Brian Legg are volunteers who carry out 'an annual survey of Heartwood's small mammal population.

00:18:57.080 --> 00:19:06.760
'Ian is licensed to handle rare and protected species, 'but we're OK with this one - the common wood mouse.'

00:19:07.200 --> 00:19:08.360
Here's the next one.

00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:09.360
You've camouflaged it well in there.

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:12.560
Well, that's the aim.

00:19:11.880 --> 00:19:21.160
'These are humane traps containing bedding 'and food to keep each animal comfortable.'

00:19:20.400 --> 00:19:22.280
Ooh, is that a vole?

00:19:21.720 --> 00:19:23.040
It is a vole.

00:19:22.080 --> 00:19:24.400
15 and a half grams.

00:19:28.480 --> 00:19:29.920
So I'll just release it.

00:19:29.440 --> 00:19:30.600
Lovely.

00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:39.560
Even in the small amount of time we've been monitoring here, we've seen a bank vole and a wood mouse so what does that variation in small mammals tell us about the ecosystem here?

00:19:39.280 --> 00:19:55.640
It tells us that we're already providing all the food and the cover that they need so they can thrive and the numbers can build up and what happens then, of course, is that the birds of prey come in as well so we've got populations of barn owls, of kestrels and we get the short-eared owls coming in in the winter.

00:19:54.800 --> 00:20:04.000
'Volunteers like Brian and Ian 'really are the beating heart of this project, 'creating a place for all to enjoy - both big and small.'

00:20:04.760 --> 00:20:22.880
A field that's slowly transforming into woodland might look to you a bit spindly and scruffy, but nature is filling this place with insects, with birdlife and with mammals, and it's wonderful to think that future generations might look up into the canopy and wonder how it all began.

00:20:29.960 --> 00:20:39.640
'Now, during the summer, we asked some well-known faces, 'from athletes... '..to comedians...'

00:20:38.760 --> 00:20:44.960
'..Actresses... 'to chefs...'

00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:45.560
Bon appetit!

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:50.560
'..Which area of our magnificent countryside was special to them.'

00:20:59.280 --> 00:21:11.840
This week, chef Tony Singh takes us to Loch Awe in Western Scotland where he found his passion for fresh produce.

00:21:12.280 --> 00:21:14.400
Wow!

00:21:13.560 --> 00:21:15.960
Still beautiful, eh?

00:21:15.880 --> 00:21:24.640
I remember coming here 28 years ago, but it just feels so welcoming.

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:38.560
I was a YTS chef when I was 18 and that led to working here at Ardanaiseig Hotel and that was the first time, basically, I'd left home.

00:21:38.400 --> 00:21:46.720
The thing I always remember that sticks in my mind was the loch just round the corner.

00:21:45.880 --> 00:21:49.440
It was so stunning and...

00:21:48.640 --> 00:21:50.040
Wow, there you go.

00:21:49.200 --> 00:21:52.080
That stayed with me forever.

00:21:58.280 --> 00:22:03.880
It just feels exactly the same.

00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:06.200
The kitchen there, which was great.

00:22:05.240 --> 00:22:10.080
The first kitchen I ever worked in that had windows, it was just phenomenal.

00:22:14.920 --> 00:22:19.640
I wondered why people went out camping or climbing mountains.

00:22:19.120 --> 00:22:23.080
"It's a mountain, yeah."

00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:23.880
And when you're here, you're intoxicated by it.

00:22:23.200 --> 00:22:30.480
I went over to the islands, I wanted to go up there, I wanted to go trekking and everything so I was champing at the bit to go out and experience everything.

00:22:31.280 --> 00:22:34.240
This is the old boathouse.

00:22:36.080 --> 00:22:42.480
This is where I first cast off on my first fishing trip and what an adventure that was!

00:22:45.560 --> 00:22:47.920
You fancy some fishing, Tony?

00:22:47.280 --> 00:22:48.840
Definitely!

00:22:51.760 --> 00:22:56.680
I came out and it was beautiful, it was like this.

00:22:55.920 --> 00:23:03.920
So we thought we'd go out for a couple of hours, hopefully catch a trout and put it on the menu.

00:23:03.000 --> 00:23:10.720
Fishing rods out, casting off and then the weather changed, just like that.

00:23:09.840 --> 00:23:21.520
Rain came in, it started getting choppy, panicked, water was coming in so we tried to head straight back to the shore through the waves and it just got worse and we ended up about a mile and a half away.

00:23:20.640 --> 00:23:32.240
We had to trudge back to the hotel with the engine, late for work, no fish, soaked, got into trouble, then we had to go back and tie off the boat.

00:23:31.480 --> 00:23:32.840
It was a nightmare.

00:23:31.880 --> 00:23:34.800
But it never put me off going back out or this view or anything.

00:23:34.040 --> 00:23:40.920
So here I am again and hopefully we'll catch something this time.

00:23:47.400 --> 00:23:49.600
Better down the middle there if it's going to snag up.

00:23:48.640 --> 00:23:49.760
Luck's out!

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:55.280
It's not going to happen.

00:23:54.480 --> 00:23:56.800
Well, that's fishing.

00:23:55.880 --> 00:23:57.000
Shall we head back?

00:23:56.120 --> 00:23:57.600
That's not a bad idea.

00:23:56.760 --> 00:23:58.120
Come on, then.

00:24:02.400 --> 00:24:12.240
Everybody talks about Scotland's larder being the best in the world and it is, but it was coming to Ardanaiseig that really etched it into my psyche.

00:24:11.280 --> 00:24:26.120
The produce on our doorstep, the butcher coming up with the lamb that he got from the farmer that he knew, catching trout, the guys going picking wild mushrooms or berries...

00:24:25.400 --> 00:24:29.360
There we go, look at that.

00:24:29.320 --> 00:24:34.080
Hedgehog mushrooms - we've got some there.

00:24:33.240 --> 00:24:38.280
If you're not sure, never eat anything, eh?

00:24:37.800 --> 00:24:40.280
We've got a fantastic cep there.

00:24:40.160 --> 00:24:45.120
It's been eaten a little bit.

00:24:44.320 --> 00:24:57.160
They used to be called penny buns or ceps, you get smaller ones, but this was an eye opener because, back in the city, we were getting produce and it was great and it was fresh and it was lovely, but I didn't connect.

00:24:56.200 --> 00:25:03.640
I didn't add one and one together, but now, when we were out and picking it fresh and seeing it, it was just like a revelation.

00:25:02.760 --> 00:25:04.240
It was fantastic.

00:25:05.600 --> 00:25:09.040
And it was free so everybody was a winner.

00:25:12.280 --> 00:25:16.840
The things that you'd pick up, it had a really profound effect on me.

00:25:17.720 --> 00:25:25.440
I've got a recipe from a wee coffee shop in town for scones and I still use that.

00:25:24.840 --> 00:25:31.200
These things do stick with you cos it always takes you back to good times.

00:25:37.160 --> 00:25:38.320
Lovely.

00:25:37.400 --> 00:25:38.640
In the pocket!

00:25:48.960 --> 00:25:54.000
This is it, this is another amazing memory.

00:25:53.080 --> 00:25:54.680
This is elemental.

00:25:53.800 --> 00:26:03.080
We've got fire, we've got water, we've got some fantastic brown trout that we're going to do justice to with just a little bit of spice and cooked on the fire.

00:26:02.280 --> 00:26:03.480
This is just...

00:26:02.600 --> 00:26:03.800
You can't get better.

00:26:02.920 --> 00:26:05.520
Look at the view.

00:26:06.600 --> 00:26:11.200
So we've got a fantastic brownie here.

00:26:10.440 --> 00:26:11.840
Look at that, beautiful!

00:26:13.520 --> 00:26:18.760
And we're just going to cook it on the embers of the fire so we'll make it a little pocket.

00:26:20.120 --> 00:26:25.640
Into that, we've got some fantastic thyme from the garden.

00:26:26.080 --> 00:26:36.640
Just a little bit of cinnamon in there, some garlic cloves, some white wine, some harissa paste, lemon.

00:26:39.560 --> 00:26:41.400
Make sure you get right in there.

00:26:47.160 --> 00:26:49.280
Rapeseed oil.

00:26:48.400 --> 00:26:53.680
The steam and the juices in there are going to make the sauce for the fish.

00:26:58.640 --> 00:27:01.560
And that's it - a Viking boat.

00:27:00.920 --> 00:27:03.440
A fit burial for the brown trout.

00:27:02.960 --> 00:27:04.400
We just want the embers there.

00:27:27.680 --> 00:27:31.840
This is it.

00:27:30.960 --> 00:27:42.760
This season I spent in Ardanaiseig away from home when I was 18... ..in this amazing countryside...

00:27:43.400 --> 00:27:48.320
This made me want to be chef.

00:27:47.600 --> 00:27:51.840
This was it - this fantastic raw produce that we had.

00:27:51.120 --> 00:27:56.160
Words can't describe it, you have to feel it and it's just brilliant.

00:27:55.600 --> 00:27:57.000
Bon appetit!

00:28:03.520 --> 00:28:12.800
Earlier, we heard about the huge cuts in bus services which are leaving those in rural areas increasingly isolated.

00:28:11.840 --> 00:28:15.600
But could community-run initiatives be the answer to the transport crisis?

00:28:15.240 --> 00:28:17.160
Tom's been finding out.

00:28:25.840 --> 00:28:33.920
The mighty Yorkshire Dales - a landscape to be explored.

00:28:40.840 --> 00:28:54.480
It certainly is an inspiring place to walk, but with deep, sweeping cuts to bus services here and across the country, connecting our rural towns and villages by public transport is becoming increasingly difficult.

00:28:56.360 --> 00:28:57.480
Hello!

00:28:56.600 --> 00:28:58.120
Hello.

00:28:57.200 --> 00:28:58.600
Can I have a day return, please?

00:29:03.960 --> 00:29:14.120
Here in North Yorkshire, though, residents have started to fight back and have gone as far as providing and effectively running a bus service themselves.

00:29:15.360 --> 00:29:16.720
It's got a bit gloomy out there, John.

00:29:15.920 --> 00:29:18.280
I'm quite glad to be on a bus.

00:29:17.400 --> 00:29:21.640
Yes, and it's almost turning into the night bus, isn't it?

00:29:22.680 --> 00:29:32.040
Started by ramblers, the DalesBus was set up to provide much needed access to the Yorkshire Dales National Park and surrounding communities.

00:29:31.080 --> 00:29:34.760
John Disney is one of the directors of the service.

00:29:36.600 --> 00:29:39.600
We started off with one service.

00:29:38.720 --> 00:29:43.160
We've now grown so that now we're running about 13 different services.

00:29:42.440 --> 00:29:49.160
Many of them have got dedicated drivers, like Keith who's driving our bus today.

00:29:48.400 --> 00:29:53.000
And why did you feel so strongly that this was an important thing to do?

00:29:52.160 --> 00:29:58.960
We basically felt that the Dales should not just be accessible to car users.

00:29:58.080 --> 00:30:09.440
There's seven million visitors to York every year, many of those come from overseas, but actually relatively few of them venture out into the Dales because they just don't know how to actually get there.

00:30:12.560 --> 00:30:22.200
As council-operated services in the area have been cut, DalesBus has seen passenger numbers increase.

00:30:22.920 --> 00:30:30.160
The big benefit for me is if I get the bus I can go for a long walk and end up somewhere else and then get the bus back home again.

00:30:30.160 --> 00:30:32.320
It's wonderful freedom and the flexibility.

00:30:31.520 --> 00:30:36.280
You get off where you want and stay for the rest of the day.

00:30:35.560 --> 00:30:37.480
They are a lifeline.

00:30:36.880 --> 00:30:43.640
I live in a little village and without our bus we would be absolutely devastated.

00:30:42.920 --> 00:30:45.360
And how often do you think you take it?

00:30:44.400 --> 00:30:45.640
Use the buses?

00:30:46.240 --> 00:30:50.560
Oh, I use them at least three or four times a week.

00:30:49.840 --> 00:30:53.280
I also use it to go to hospital.

00:30:54.640 --> 00:31:06.480
No longer just providing a service solely for ramblers, the buses have become vital for many over-60s without access to a car.

00:31:05.600 --> 00:31:15.600
On many routes, buses are required by law to be free for those with a concessionary pass, but for the Dalesbus this has come at a price.

00:31:15.160 --> 00:31:22.120
The Saturday service we operate between Skipton and Harrogate is doing marvellously in terms of passengers.

00:31:21.160 --> 00:31:27.040
But the revenue still falls about £130 a day short of the operating costs.

00:31:26.160 --> 00:31:28.480
Because most of them aren't paying.

00:31:27.600 --> 00:31:32.520
Yeah, about 75% of the passengers on that route are concessionary pass holders.

00:31:31.680 --> 00:31:47.600
Councils reimburse bus operators for passengers who use free bus passes, but many are now cutting the level of that reimbursement and community enterprises like the Dalesbus are now struggling to survive.

00:31:50.840 --> 00:32:00.320
It seems the unintentional cost of free public transport is destroying the service for those that need it most.

00:31:59.520 --> 00:32:04.040
So why aren't councils able to provide more money to help?

00:32:07.000 --> 00:32:10.440
Anything catching your eye this morning, Jonathan?

00:32:09.480 --> 00:32:15.840
'Jonathan Carr-West is the Chief Executive 'of a local government think tank.

00:32:14.920 --> 00:32:20.200
'He believes councils simply don't have the money 'to spend on subsidising buses.'

00:32:20.440 --> 00:32:24.800
Why is public transport, particularly buses, being hit so hard in rural areas?

00:32:24.760 --> 00:32:31.840
I think what people have to realise is what a tough time local governments are having financially and some of the difficult decisions they have to make.

00:32:30.920 --> 00:32:39.760
They have lost about 40% of their funding from central government and 70% of what is left they have to spend on legal obligations.

00:32:38.920 --> 00:32:47.880
We all want vulnerable children protected, we all want our elderly relatives to be cared for - most people, I think, feel that that is more important than transport.

00:32:47.040 --> 00:32:54.520
We have to accept there is a reality that there will be less money to spend on bus services because there is just less money, so we need to spend what we do have smarter.

00:32:55.920 --> 00:33:03.720
'Ultimately, what councils spend their money on is up to them 'and there are clearly hard decisions to make.'

00:33:03.240 --> 00:33:05.720
So what is the solution, do you think, for public transport in the countryside?

00:33:05.760 --> 00:33:14.400
I think there are things that local government can do and that some councils are doing around the country to use the little resources they have left more creatively.

00:33:13.600 --> 00:33:22.320
You can concentrate on key bus services, you can wrap around volunteer services, you can have on-demand minibus services.

00:33:21.840 --> 00:33:28.800
And in the end, when you think about transport, it's not just about vehicles going from one place to another, it's about how you connect a whole community.

00:33:33.640 --> 00:33:43.960
When it comes to protecting rural bus services, local volunteers and business brains can achieve a lot.

00:33:43.000 --> 00:33:53.560
But they are always going to need some help from central government and we should find out what they're planning when the Buses Bill is published in the next few months.

00:33:55.360 --> 00:34:06.920
I am in West Hertfordshire meeting a farming family in the Chilterns and finding out more about their latest venture called pressed rapeseed oil.

00:34:06.120 --> 00:34:14.520
Entrepreneurship runs in Simon's blood - the family started off selling their produce from a stall by the side of the road.

00:34:13.560 --> 00:34:19.080
These days, it's from their farm shop and tearooms.

00:34:18.560 --> 00:34:27.040
So, where better to put Simon's rapeseed oil to the test and see how it stands up against its Mediterranean cousin, olive oil?

00:34:27.239 --> 00:34:31.960
Simon's wife, Jenny, is helping set up our taste test.

00:34:32.520 --> 00:34:35.960
Jenny, I have one quite literally hot off the press here.

00:34:35.080 --> 00:34:36.600
Brilliant.

00:34:35.960 --> 00:34:41.520
So we are going to do a bit of a taste test here, aren't we, with the customers?

00:34:43.400 --> 00:34:44.600
So shall we just tip it in there?

00:34:43.639 --> 00:34:44.719
I'll see how we go.

00:34:43.840 --> 00:34:45.159
OK.

00:34:44.199 --> 00:34:45.719
I won't be long.

00:34:46.040 --> 00:34:47.639
Righty-ho.

00:34:47.000 --> 00:34:48.239
Hello.

00:34:47.280 --> 00:34:50.639
Would you like to try a bit of this?

00:34:50.360 --> 00:34:52.560
What is it?

00:34:52.120 --> 00:34:53.800
Which is which?

00:34:53.199 --> 00:34:54.679
Well, you tell me.

00:34:54.000 --> 00:34:55.840
That one.

00:34:55.120 --> 00:34:57.080
Can I interrupt?

00:34:56.520 --> 00:34:58.040
You certainly can, Matt.

00:34:57.160 --> 00:34:59.920
Would you have a little taste and see which one you think is best?

00:34:59.840 --> 00:35:02.480
All we are asking you to do is dip that bit of bread into the oil and see which one you prefer.

00:35:04.920 --> 00:35:08.520
Well, they are just two completely different flavours, aren't they?

00:35:08.560 --> 00:35:12.240
But that one is so much nicer.

00:35:12.800 --> 00:35:16.200
That's the rapeseed.

00:35:15.240 --> 00:35:16.800
..

00:35:15.840 --> 00:35:17.080
I'd have that.

00:35:16.200 --> 00:35:17.320
I like that one best.

00:35:16.400 --> 00:35:17.560
Do you?

00:35:16.680 --> 00:35:17.920
OK.

00:35:16.960 --> 00:35:18.160
Interesting.

00:35:17.320 --> 00:35:19.600
Olive oil.

00:35:20.560 --> 00:35:21.800
I think that one.

00:35:20.920 --> 00:35:23.280
Do you know what that is?

00:35:22.320 --> 00:35:23.440
No.

00:35:22.840 --> 00:35:24.560
That's rapeseed oil.

00:35:23.600 --> 00:35:24.960
Oh!

00:35:31.680 --> 00:35:33.600
Mm, that one.

00:35:32.760 --> 00:35:34.240
You prefer the lighter, do you?

00:35:33.280 --> 00:35:34.840
I do.

00:35:33.920 --> 00:35:36.240
Have you thought about getting hold of one of these?

00:35:35.600 --> 00:35:38.400
I buy one every year.

00:35:37.560 --> 00:35:39.040
Do you?!

00:35:38.200 --> 00:35:39.400
I do.

00:35:38.640 --> 00:35:40.080
Oh, that's fantastic news.

00:35:39.320 --> 00:35:48.080
Yes, it is the Countryfile calendar sold in aid of Children In Need and if you haven't got yours yet, you need to get onto the website quickly.

00:35:47.120 --> 00:35:48.320
All the details are on there...

00:35:49.360 --> 00:35:51.200
And thank you for buying yours.

00:35:50.240 --> 00:35:51.960
That's very kind.

00:36:03.760 --> 00:36:12.440
Now, last year we asked you to tell us about your farming heroes for a brand-new Countryfile award.

00:36:11.680 --> 00:36:17.280
The response was incredible and it was a tough ask for Adam and his fellow judges to pick a winner.

00:36:18.840 --> 00:36:29.880
Now, we really agonised about this but we felt that this year's winner went above and beyond, both as a farmer and as a stalwart of her local community.

00:36:29.000 --> 00:36:31.440
The winner is Joan Bomford.

00:36:31.960 --> 00:36:45.160
Well, later, we are going to be launching Farming Hero 2016, but before that, Adam has met up with last year's winner and if she can't inspire you, nothing will.

00:36:45.880 --> 00:36:51.680
Last March, I met Joan Bomford for the very first time.

00:36:50.840 --> 00:36:56.520
She has been farming since the 1930s and at the age of 83 she is still going strong.

00:36:56.240 --> 00:36:58.000
So, tell me about your life, then.

00:36:57.120 --> 00:36:59.960
When did you start working on farms?

00:36:59.200 --> 00:37:03.600
When I was about eight, milking.

00:37:02.840 --> 00:37:07.880
The most funny thing was that father always dressed me as a lad.

00:37:07.000 --> 00:37:09.080
Why did he do that?

00:37:08.760 --> 00:37:12.720
Well, I think he wanted a lad.

00:37:12.600 --> 00:37:20.640
It was Joan's work in the community and her tireless charity work that made her a farming hero and she has still got bags of energy.

00:37:20.040 --> 00:37:25.480
It is such a waste of life to do nothing and sit around, isn't it?

00:37:24.520 --> 00:37:26.400
So make the most of it, says I.

00:37:27.120 --> 00:37:30.120
The winner is Joan Bomford.

00:37:30.240 --> 00:37:38.680
It was an honour to present Joan with Countryfile's Farming Hero Award at the BBC Food and Farming Awards last April.

00:37:38.040 --> 00:37:42.560
Since winning, Joan has attracted all sorts of attention.

00:37:41.880 --> 00:37:44.320
I am catching up with her again to find out more.

00:37:48.400 --> 00:37:56.000
As the sun rises over her farm in Worcestershire, I know there is only one place Joan will be - out in the field with her animals.

00:37:58.600 --> 00:38:01.360
There's Joan.

00:38:00.520 --> 00:38:04.240
Up with the lark, hard at work.

00:38:06.920 --> 00:38:09.200
Morning, Joan.

00:38:08.240 --> 00:38:09.480
How are you?

00:38:08.520 --> 00:38:10.000
All right, and you?

00:38:11.720 --> 00:38:13.360
Goodness me, working hard as ever!

00:38:13.160 --> 00:38:14.560
Yeah.

00:38:14.240 --> 00:38:18.640
You have been very busy since I last saw you at the Farming Awards.

00:38:17.680 --> 00:38:19.600
On the telly and radio and all sorts, isn't it?

00:38:19.400 --> 00:38:24.520
Telly, radio, we went up to London, we came down to Bristol for the prize-giving.

00:38:24.120 --> 00:38:25.360
Yeah.

00:38:24.400 --> 00:38:28.600
It has been amazing, really.

00:38:28.840 --> 00:38:31.480
And what about this, then?

00:38:31.320 --> 00:38:32.440
Yeah.

00:38:31.480 --> 00:38:32.960
Your book.

00:38:32.040 --> 00:38:35.440
I got pestered to do a book.

00:38:34.720 --> 00:38:39.400
They said my life was worth talking about, so that's what I did.

00:38:38.560 --> 00:38:40.800
It absolutely is.

00:38:40.800 --> 00:38:43.360
This is a wonderful photograph there.

00:38:42.480 --> 00:38:44.280
Yeah.

00:38:43.400 --> 00:38:46.840
It says in the book that you left school at 14.

00:38:46.680 --> 00:38:48.160
Is that right?

00:38:47.240 --> 00:38:51.440
Yeah, well, before, because for the last year I didn't go.

00:38:50.720 --> 00:38:51.840
Didn't you?

00:38:50.920 --> 00:38:55.360
I went one day, that was to pick up my books.

00:38:54.560 --> 00:38:59.440
So what would your English teacher have thought about you writing a book, then?

00:38:58.480 --> 00:38:59.800
Erm, I don't know.

00:39:00.400 --> 00:39:05.160
I expect she would be quite pleased because she did make us try and write properly, you know.

00:39:09.000 --> 00:39:12.000
And all these animals, how many ponies have you got now?

00:39:11.040 --> 00:39:13.000
Oh, about 80.

00:39:13.120 --> 00:39:15.680
And how many cattle?

00:39:14.720 --> 00:39:16.480
About the same, yeah.

00:39:15.640 --> 00:39:18.040
That is a big herd of cattle.

00:39:17.240 --> 00:39:18.600
Oh, yes.

00:39:17.760 --> 00:39:19.200
It keeps you busy.

00:39:19.000 --> 00:39:20.760
Are you coping?

00:39:20.040 --> 00:39:21.720
Yeah, just about.

00:39:20.760 --> 00:39:21.960
Wonderful.

00:39:23.640 --> 00:39:27.040
And I understand you have got some jobs to do with the cattle today.

00:39:26.280 --> 00:39:29.120
Yeah, we are going to Worcester Market with three bulls.

00:39:28.160 --> 00:39:29.400
OK.

00:39:28.520 --> 00:39:29.920
I'll give you a hand with that, shall I?

00:39:28.960 --> 00:39:30.240
Yes, OK.

00:39:29.320 --> 00:39:30.640
Let's go and get them loaded.

00:39:31.080 --> 00:39:32.400
OK.

00:39:42.800 --> 00:39:48.280
With help from Joan's son Colin, we round up the cattle.

00:39:47.480 --> 00:39:54.360
All of her stock are raised for beef and at 28 months old they are ready to go to market.

00:39:54.960 --> 00:39:57.800
83, still driving a lorry.

00:39:56.920 --> 00:39:58.240
Wonderful!

00:40:00.560 --> 00:40:04.240
With Joan at the wheel, we are off to Worcester Livestock Auction.

00:40:06.520 --> 00:40:10.640
Joan has been coming to Worcester to sell cattle for more than 75 years.

00:40:18.720 --> 00:40:20.240
Stop!

00:40:19.400 --> 00:40:20.800
Spot on.

00:40:24.600 --> 00:40:26.520
How do you mind driving on the roads nowadays?

00:40:25.560 --> 00:40:28.840
I'm all right.

00:40:28.280 --> 00:40:31.120
I'm a bit bigger than them.

00:40:30.160 --> 00:40:31.800
OK, let's get them off.

00:40:30.880 --> 00:40:32.080
Yeah, right.

00:40:41.760 --> 00:40:44.360
Joan is just handing over all the paperwork.

00:40:43.600 --> 00:40:50.160
All the cattle have their individual passports that match up to their ear tags.

00:40:49.280 --> 00:40:54.040
They go down into individual pens and then into the sale ring.

00:40:53.240 --> 00:40:54.760
They are being sold for beef today.

00:40:56.720 --> 00:41:01.200
They look nice in there, Joan, don't they?

00:41:00.360 --> 00:41:01.760
Yeah, yeah.

00:41:00.880 --> 00:41:02.960
You have got them well turned out.

00:41:02.200 --> 00:41:04.480
Yeah, it's a shame, really, they've got to go, isn't it?

00:41:04.080 --> 00:41:08.200
How do you feel about the emotions of bringing cattle to market?

00:41:07.320 --> 00:41:18.960
Well, you know it's their last journey and I suppose you get so used to them and you do miss them.

00:41:18.080 --> 00:41:19.560
Yeah, sure.

00:41:18.680 --> 00:41:22.720
But you are proud of what you are producing.

00:41:21.760 --> 00:41:23.000
Yes, that's the main thing, isn't it?

00:41:22.920 --> 00:41:24.960
Yeah, make some lovely beef, won't they?

00:41:24.000 --> 00:41:26.040
Yeah.

00:41:26.160 --> 00:41:29.240
I particularly like that number 44.

00:41:28.360 --> 00:41:30.520
That'll make a good price, won't it?

00:41:29.720 --> 00:41:32.480
Yes, that's the best one of the two, really, the three, yeah.

00:41:33.840 --> 00:41:38.080
And what sort of money do you think they will make?

00:41:37.200 --> 00:41:43.400
Erm, well, hopefully 900 to 1,000, maybe.

00:41:42.440 --> 00:41:43.840
Yeah.

00:41:43.200 --> 00:41:48.920
They might not, they might even go for 600 and it's very disappointing.

00:41:50.000 --> 00:41:53.440
Well, fingers crossed.

00:41:52.560 --> 00:41:53.680
Hopefully they'll make the right price, pay for all that diesel.

00:41:55.920 --> 00:42:00.120
Joan loves coming to the market to catch up with fellow farmers.

00:42:00.440 --> 00:42:05.000
To the locals she has always been a farming hero.

00:42:04.320 --> 00:42:06.840
You know Joan, don't you?

00:42:05.880 --> 00:42:07.920
Lovely character, isn't she?

00:42:07.080 --> 00:42:08.320
Oh, yeah, yeah.

00:42:07.360 --> 00:42:11.120
A real worker, a real worker.

00:42:10.280 --> 00:42:15.000
I don't think you could find anyone who would work as hard.

00:42:14.320 --> 00:42:16.720
She's a miracle lady, isn't she?

00:42:17.240 --> 00:42:19.160
Oh, she is a one-off.

00:42:18.240 --> 00:42:22.800
I think after they made her they broke the mould.

00:42:23.160 --> 00:42:29.320
Well, I hope I am still going that strong when I am 83.

00:42:28.680 --> 00:42:31.400
I'm closer to it than you are.

00:42:31.080 --> 00:42:35.320
I have got my fingers crossed that Joan's first steer will command a good price.

00:42:35.560 --> 00:42:37.960
Is it a bit of a gamble, bringing the cattle?

00:42:37.160 --> 00:42:40.760
Yeah, it is, because you never know what you're going to get.

00:42:41.640 --> 00:42:46.040
The cattle need to sell for at least £600 each for Joan to make any money on them at all.

00:42:46.960 --> 00:42:48.840
This auctioneer rattles through it.

00:42:47.960 --> 00:42:49.640
I can hardly keep up with him.

00:42:48.680 --> 00:42:50.040
He doesn't stop, does he?

00:42:53.600 --> 00:42:56.240
The first one is sold in seconds.

00:42:59.760 --> 00:43:04.280
1.67 a kilo, live weight.

00:43:03.920 --> 00:43:05.240
Yeah.

00:43:04.280 --> 00:43:06.480
So I can't do the maths, but that's over £1,000.

00:43:05.520 --> 00:43:06.840
Yeah.

00:43:08.520 --> 00:43:10.040
Very good.

00:43:09.200 --> 00:43:10.520
Mm.

00:43:09.640 --> 00:43:11.400
'That has more than made the trip worthwhile.

00:43:10.480 --> 00:43:13.280
'A very good price indeed.

00:43:17.560 --> 00:43:18.720
'Here comes the next one.'

00:43:17.760 --> 00:43:21.520
This next one, you said before it was your favourite one.

00:43:20.640 --> 00:43:21.800
You reckon this one is the best one of the three.

00:43:20.880 --> 00:43:22.160
Yeah.

00:43:23.080 --> 00:43:27.160
So that's just over 600 kilos as well.

00:43:26.360 --> 00:43:28.240
That's nearly 1,100 quid on that one, isn't it?

00:43:27.280 --> 00:43:28.440
Yeah.

00:43:27.600 --> 00:43:29.000
Very good.

00:43:31.160 --> 00:43:32.600
'Another good price.

00:43:31.760 --> 00:43:36.200
The third one also sells for something similar.'

00:43:35.760 --> 00:43:37.720
They've done well, I think, haven't they?

00:43:36.920 --> 00:43:38.200
Yeah, they have.

00:43:37.240 --> 00:43:38.520
Somewhere in the region of...

00:43:37.720 --> 00:43:39.720
They averaged over £1,000 apiece, I'd have thought.

00:43:39.120 --> 00:43:40.640
Yeah.

00:43:39.800 --> 00:43:41.600
Are you pleased?

00:43:40.840 --> 00:43:42.200
It's a good average, yeah.

00:43:41.240 --> 00:43:43.240
Well done.

00:43:44.760 --> 00:43:46.400
And what about you, are you going to retire soon?

00:43:45.920 --> 00:43:47.400
No.

00:43:48.000 --> 00:43:51.280
Going to stick with it.

00:43:50.480 --> 00:43:51.800
What's the point?

00:43:50.920 --> 00:43:52.480
What would I do?

00:43:52.520 --> 00:43:54.280
I don't know, write another book.

00:43:54.720 --> 00:43:58.560
Oh, I can do that in my spare time.

00:43:58.000 --> 00:44:00.240
What a remarkable lady you are.

00:44:04.800 --> 00:44:15.640
Every time I meet Joan I feel moved by her motivation and enthusiasm and there's no doubt in my mind that she deserved to win the Countryfile Farming Hero award last year.

00:44:15.000 --> 00:44:26.520
And this year we are launching the awards again, so we want to hear from you about those special people in the countryside who go above and beyond.

00:44:26.760 --> 00:44:33.760
The award is for a farmer or farming family who have made a difference through their heroic actions.

00:44:32.880 --> 00:44:43.440
The judges want to hear about farmers who have come to the rescue of others, man or beast, at a time of need.

00:44:42.880 --> 00:44:50.160
They could have organised emergency animal housing for their fellow farmer, have helped their neighbour when times were bleak.

00:44:49.360 --> 00:44:56.880
We will celebrate the achievements of truly remarkable people who make our countryside a better place.

00:44:56.280 --> 00:45:03.520
Our winner will be someone who has gone above and beyond to help their farming friends and neighbours and of whom we can all be proud.

00:45:04.120 --> 00:45:12.600
If you know someone like Joan here who might be a farming hero then let me know so that we can thank them and recognise them.

00:45:12.240 --> 00:45:14.400
And for the nominations...

00:45:13.600 --> 00:45:17.960
You better get your skates on because there's only two weeks to go, starting from today.

00:45:20.760 --> 00:45:27.080
Nominations close at midnight on January 24th, so names sent in after that won't be considered.

00:45:26.400 --> 00:45:32.600
Remember, if you are watching us on demand, nominations may have already closed.

00:45:31.840 --> 00:45:34.080
Details, including terms and conditions, are on our website.

00:45:43.560 --> 00:45:48.520
I'm only 20 or so miles as the crow flies from Marble Arch.

00:45:47.680 --> 00:45:51.880
I could be slap-bang in the middle of London in no time.

00:45:51.120 --> 00:45:54.560
But the city feels far, far away.

00:45:56.760 --> 00:46:09.480
Rural tranquillity on the doorstep of the capital is a commuter's dream combination, and a farming county with prime arable land - Hertfordshire has a lot to recommend it.

00:46:08.880 --> 00:46:13.120
No wonder it's not short of a few bob.

00:46:14.880 --> 00:46:22.200
It's one of the richest counties with some of the highest house prices in the UK.

00:46:22.960 --> 00:46:30.720
The three most common uses of land in Hertfordshire are towns and cities, farming and transport.

00:46:29.800 --> 00:46:33.720
But what could the fourth be in such a well-off place?

00:46:33.720 --> 00:46:34.960
Golf.

00:46:36.760 --> 00:46:41.360
Hertfordshire has 70 golf courses spread all over the county.

00:46:44.920 --> 00:46:53.520
When you think about golf, if you picture immaculate fairways and perfectly-manicured grass, well, you wouldn't be far wrong.

00:46:52.640 --> 00:47:00.400
But here at Mid-Herts Golf Course, they are bringing in something a whole lot wilder.

00:46:59.800 --> 00:47:09.440
Heathland, a heather-clad habitat we associate with windswept moors and untamed uplands, not the Home Counties.

00:47:08.800 --> 00:47:15.480
Hertfordshire has lost 97% of its heathland since 1940.

00:47:15.840 --> 00:47:26.440
Tim Hill from Herts And Middlesex Wildlife Trust is trying to bring it back.

00:47:26.280 --> 00:47:29.920
So, that habitat once was here in relative abundance.

00:47:29.120 --> 00:47:39.520
It was widespread but due to changes in land use, loss of grazing, it means that a lot of trees were now coming, a lot of scrub, and it's shaded out the heather.

00:47:38.760 --> 00:47:42.040
But why golf courses?

00:47:41.240 --> 00:47:50.360
In Hertfordshire, there's over 8,500 acres of golf courses and most of the heathland that is remaining is on those golf courses.

00:47:50.000 --> 00:47:53.960
And there's some of this heather here.

00:47:53.880 --> 00:47:55.280
Let's have a look at this.

00:47:54.320 --> 00:47:55.800
It's really thriving.

00:47:55.960 --> 00:48:00.800
This is some of the heather that has been restored and conserved over the last ten years or so.

00:48:00.240 --> 00:48:04.080
And how have you gone about getting it here?

00:48:03.440 --> 00:48:14.320
Well, it has been a lot of hard work, a lot of advice given by the Wildlife Trust to make it happen, but primarily it's down to the groundsman and his staff that have managed to make it look as good as this.

00:48:16.800 --> 00:48:21.520
Jody Wilson is the groundsman at Mid-Herts Golf Club.

00:48:20.920 --> 00:48:28.320
This isn't just routine maintenance, this is a carefully planned operation to help reintroduce the heather.

00:48:28.440 --> 00:48:30.480
Jody, stop, stop.

00:48:29.600 --> 00:48:32.080
The storm of leaves there!

00:48:32.560 --> 00:48:34.080
Quite a noisy business, isn't it?

00:48:33.120 --> 00:48:34.600
Very noisy.

00:48:35.800 --> 00:48:42.200
I know you guys are incredibly neat and tidy but this seems like quite a big step, making sure every leaf is gone.

00:48:41.800 --> 00:48:50.240
Yeah, unfortunately with the heather, it hates organic matter and clearing the leaf is essential for it to grow for us.

00:48:49.640 --> 00:48:52.680
Otherwise it gets so smothered, and we try and encourage the baby heather to come through as well.

00:48:51.960 --> 00:48:53.680
Do you fancy giving us a hand?

00:49:06.640 --> 00:49:11.480
It's mine and Jody's job to shift the leaves and then the tractor comes along to pick them up.

00:49:14.240 --> 00:49:16.320
It's really effective.

00:49:16.240 --> 00:49:19.120
But getting the heather here in the first place is a challenge in itself.

00:49:21.040 --> 00:49:34.000
Basically, what we've done here, we've lifted some turf because obviously we are trying to get down to the base of the heather seed that was originally there sort of 50, 60 years ago, and then we bring the heather brashings in, which has got lots of seed in, and then we sprinkle away.

00:49:33.040 --> 00:49:38.240
Hopefully, in a couple of years, or perhaps in a year's time, we will see some really good heather growth.

00:49:44.960 --> 00:49:47.920
But what do the club's members make of this added obstacle?

00:49:50.240 --> 00:49:56.160
Pippa Legg has been playing golf here for 25 years.

00:49:55.280 --> 00:50:01.280
Conservation aside, how is this heathland restoration good for the game?

00:50:00.440 --> 00:50:12.680
Well, the heather, particularly when it's in flower, is beautiful but it also presents a real hazard that golfers have to either manage to get out of or avoid.

00:50:11.800 --> 00:50:19.800
And then these fine grasses, you can find the golf ball quite easily but you still need a fair amount of skill to get the golf ball back out of the rough and onto the fairway.

00:50:20.800 --> 00:50:23.160
I have never played golf but...

00:50:23.040 --> 00:50:24.200
Right, OK.

00:50:23.240 --> 00:50:25.840
..I have got a club and I wouldn't mind you teaching me.

00:50:24.880 --> 00:50:26.040
Right, what do I do?

00:50:28.160 --> 00:50:29.280
Keep your eye on the ball.

00:50:28.400 --> 00:50:35.200
Keep your head still and just watch the ball, and then just take a nice swing.

00:50:34.400 --> 00:50:37.240
Ah, well, that was a practice swing, yeah?

00:50:38.840 --> 00:50:40.800
Oh, I just don't have this!

00:50:43.080 --> 00:50:45.040
Head still, swish the ground.

00:50:44.240 --> 00:50:47.240
Oh, I can't even make contact!

00:50:46.280 --> 00:50:47.640
There you are, you see?

00:50:57.880 --> 00:51:07.480
While Ellie has been searching out small mammals in the woodlands of Hertfordshire, I have been following the production of some of the county's home-grown rapeseed oil.

00:51:07.960 --> 00:51:16.480
It has already proved popular amongst the customers of the farm shop, but it can boast another high-profile fan.

00:51:15.640 --> 00:51:30.520
Michelin-starred French chef Jean-Christophe Novelli made Hertfordshire his home more than a decade ago and opened up his own cookery academy.

00:51:32.040 --> 00:51:38.760
It is here that he shares his gastronomic know-how and passion for local produce.

00:51:39.440 --> 00:51:44.960
And I am very excited because I have been invited for dinner, and I haven't come empty handed.

00:51:48.440 --> 00:51:50.240
Jean-Christophe, how are you?

00:51:51.720 --> 00:51:55.440
Listen, I brought you a bottle, OK?

00:51:54.560 --> 00:51:56.360
It's not wine, but I think you're going to like this a little bit more.

00:51:56.680 --> 00:51:57.920
I will, for sure.

00:51:57.080 --> 00:51:59.600
This is absolutely fantastic.

00:52:00.480 --> 00:52:04.200
First on the menu are roasted winter vegetables.

00:52:04.200 --> 00:52:09.320
I have got beetroots, turnips, swedes.

00:52:08.360 --> 00:52:10.080
These things, the real food of earth, basically.

00:52:09.280 --> 00:52:13.560
I want to have a little bit of this oil coating around all my vegetables.

00:52:13.400 --> 00:52:19.280
Trained in traditional French cuisine, in recent years, Jean-Christophe has focused more on healthy eating...

00:52:22.760 --> 00:52:24.840
Just roll everything around inside.

00:52:23.920 --> 00:52:30.040
This will only have a quarter of the saturated fat of any olive oil on the planet.

00:52:29.320 --> 00:52:36.640
..And now uses barely any butter or salt in his dishes, so rapeseed oil is the ideal substitute.

00:52:39.120 --> 00:52:40.760
That goes in the oven.

00:52:39.920 --> 00:52:42.440
The only thing I am waiting now is the smell.

00:52:41.560 --> 00:52:46.080
Next up, cranberries left over from the festive season are put to good use.

00:52:45.320 --> 00:52:50.440
A spoonful of honey, spices and a drop of rapeseed oil bring out their vibrant flavour.

00:52:49.480 --> 00:52:50.840
Just a touch.

00:52:49.920 --> 00:52:51.120
Please go for it.

00:52:58.800 --> 00:53:02.760
Oh, my word!

00:53:02.360 --> 00:53:04.560
That is a burst of flavour.

00:53:05.480 --> 00:53:07.680
Next on the menu, scallops.

00:53:06.720 --> 00:53:13.880
Usually seared in butter, Jean-Christophe's healthy approach means he uses just a sheen of rapeseed oil.

00:53:12.960 --> 00:53:14.280
If you just do it like this, just nice and easy.

00:53:13.360 --> 00:53:14.680
And you know what is funny?

00:53:16.360 --> 00:53:19.840
You do it on one side only.

00:53:19.040 --> 00:53:20.840
OK.

00:53:22.280 --> 00:53:28.720
Do you know, I love your connection to the food.

00:53:27.960 --> 00:53:29.800
I just like food and that's it.

00:53:28.840 --> 00:53:36.120
If you really want to have a bit of the sea, don't put salt on it because the fish, or the shellfish... ..will speak for himself.

00:53:35.720 --> 00:53:37.120
Now, look at that.

00:53:39.360 --> 00:53:43.040
And those scallops should not take longer than that.

00:53:42.880 --> 00:53:45.960
So they have all gone rapeseed oil side down.

00:53:45.160 --> 00:53:46.400
That's right.

00:53:46.840 --> 00:53:50.560
The secret to scallops is keeping it simple.

00:53:49.680 --> 00:53:54.160
Hot pans, bing, bang, out.

00:53:53.200 --> 00:53:55.760
A drizzle of oil and voila.

00:53:56.160 --> 00:53:57.920
And that's it, it's done.

00:53:57.040 --> 00:54:00.400
I see I've chosen the right moment to come in!

00:53:59.680 --> 00:54:01.320
It smells lovely.

00:54:00.440 --> 00:54:02.000
Bonjour, very nice to meet you.

00:54:01.120 --> 00:54:03.760
Right, I'll just put that there and that down there.

00:54:04.520 --> 00:54:06.120
And are we be ready to eat now?

00:54:14.800 --> 00:54:17.040
Shall I slide down this way so I can get close to the plate?

00:54:23.800 --> 00:54:24.920
Lead beautifully.

00:54:24.040 --> 00:54:25.560
Mmm.

00:54:26.080 --> 00:54:27.320
Verdict?

00:54:26.360 --> 00:54:27.600
Wow!

00:54:26.640 --> 00:54:28.640
What a lovely flavour.

00:54:28.160 --> 00:54:31.440
Lots of sharpness too, which is nice.

00:54:30.720 --> 00:54:34.880
You are very lucky, lots of people have been doing a rapeseed oil test today and this is the finest one of all.

00:54:36.280 --> 00:54:38.120
Oh, yeah, that is good.

00:54:38.760 --> 00:54:42.280
Isn't that a rewarding end?

00:54:41.440 --> 00:54:44.120
Thank you so much, honestly, for all the tips.

00:54:43.640 --> 00:54:44.960
But that is it for this week.

00:54:44.160 --> 00:54:48.040
Next week we're going to be in Gloucestershire.

00:54:47.160 --> 00:54:48.440
So until then, bye-bye.

00:54:48.520 --> 00:54:51.640
You can come and do the catering, if you want.

00:54:53.000 --> 00:54:54.440
Brilliant idea!

00:55:36.960 --> 00:55:38.040
Let your New Year start with a bang and visit an explosive new China.

00:55:37.280 --> 00:55:38.520
Look what China has achieved.

00:55:37.560 --> 00:55:38.680
Celebrate a country 4,000 years in the making.

00:55:37.880 --> 00:55:39.080
China begins here.

00:55:39.200 --> 00:55:40.320
Getting out into the streets and seeing what it's all about.

00:55:40.080 --> 00:55:41.160
And see this little-known nation in a brand-new light.

00:55:40.280 --> 00:55:41.400
China Season...

00:55:41.520 --> 00:55:42.760
So distinctive and so brilliant.

00:55:41.800 --> 00:55:44.240
..Starts with...

00:55:59.800 --> 00:56:00.880
It all started with the discovery of one enormous thigh bone.

00:56:00.360 --> 00:56:02.040
It's like a paleontological crime scene.

00:56:13.120 --> 00:56:14.160
Join Sir David Attenborough on the ultimate detective journey... ..to discover if this really is the biggest animal to ever walk the Earth.

00:56:13.760 --> 00:56:14.800
Well!

00:56:21.840 --> 00:56:24.600
Absolutely amazing!
